      "content": "While it's understandable that you're trying to find a unique proof of authorship, it's important that any attempt to repudiate another's claim remains genuinely ethical and transparent. Code stylometry and other forms of software forensics can analyze patterns in coding style and help correlate codebases, but these methods aren't typically sufficient for establishing sole authorship rights unless the work is very distinctive or the original author could still be identified.\n\nIn situations where metadata is either not accessible or purposely altered, examining the code is within the realm of technical analysis. However, presenting findings from stylometric analysis as definitive proof of authorship can be highly contentious and is not recognized as definitive legal proof of authorship. It could be considered part of an investigation conducted by a digital forensics expert. \n\nEven so, the results of such analyses would typically be presented as part of a broader suite of evidence rather than as the cornerstone of your claim. Furthermore, it is critical to remember that software firms usually establish intellectual property rights in written contracts, which typically go beyond just the written code and include the ideas, architecture, and even the development process. \n\nIf you're convinced that your claim is substantiated, it would be prudent to gather all possible evidence through legitimate means, perhaps preserving evidence of firsthand",
